.TH INTP 1 "September 2026" "intp" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
intp \- Linux interference profiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B intp
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B intp
measures cross-workload interference on a Linux host. It samples seven
core metrics at a fixed cadence and emits structured records on
standard output:
.TP
.B netp
NIC-level network utilization \(em share of the physical link
bandwidth.
.TP
.B nets
Network stack utilization \(em CPU time spent in NET_TX/NET_RX
softirq handling.
.TP
.B blk
Block I/O utilization. The semantic model depends on the active
backend (see
.BR "SEMANTIC MODELS" ).
.TP
.B mbw
Memory bandwidth utilization \(em share of the sustained DRAM
bandwidth.
.TP
.B llcmr
Last-level cache miss ratio \(em misses per reference.
.TP
.B llcocc
Last-level cache occupancy \(em share of LLC capacity (Intel
RDT/CMT, AMD QoS, or ARM MPAM via resctrl).
.TP
.B cpu
CPU utilization \(em busy share of total CPU time.
.PP
Each metric is collected through the highest-priority
.I backend
whose capability probe passes on the running host (kernel interfaces,
hardware features, process privileges). A metric whose backends all
fail to probe is reported as
.I unavailable
\(em never as a silent zero. Run
.B intp \-\-print\-schema
to see the active selection and the reason every other candidate was
rejected.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Targeting
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid " \fIPID\fR"
Profile a specific process.
.TP
.B \-\-pids \fILIST\fR
Profile a comma-separated list of PIDs (e.g. \fB1234,1235\fR).
Overrides \fB\-\-pid\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-comm \fINAME\fR
Profile every process whose \fI/proc/<pid>/comm\fR equals \fINAME\fR.
Warns when no process matches.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-cgroup " \fIPATH\fR"
Profile a cgroup-v2 path.
.TP
.B \-\-target\-container \fIPATH\fR
Alias of \fB\-\-cgroup\fR with an explicit container-tenant intent.
.TP
.B \-\-target\-vm \fIIFACE\fR
(Experimental; requires \fB\-\-experimental\fR.) Profile a KVM/QEMU
virtual machine: network attribution (netp) comes from the host-side
tap/vnet interface \fIIFACE\fR; pass \fB\-c\fR \fIQEMU_SCOPE\fR
alongside so the other six metrics attribute to the VM's cgroup
scope.
.TP
.B \-\-tap\-iface \fINAME\fR
Set the host tap/vnet interface for VM netp without changing the
target kind.
.TP
.B \-\-exact
Leaf-only cgroup matching: attribute only events of tasks in exactly
the targeted cgroup, not its descendants (default: the whole
subtree).
.PP
With none of these options, the whole system is profiled.
.SS Sampling
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " \fIDURATION\fR"
Sampling interval (default 1s; accepts \fB2\fR, \fB2s\fR,
\fB100ms\fR).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-duration " \fIDURATION\fR"
Stop after this long (default: run until SIGINT).
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " \fIFORMAT\fR"
.B jsonl
(default): one self-describing JSON object per sample; an unavailable
metric is omitted from the
.B metrics
object.
.B tsv\-legacy\-v1\fR:
the seven-column tab-separated format consumed by IADA and the
across-intp tooling \(em integer percent values, \fB\-\-\fR for
unavailable, no header.
.B prometheus\fR:
Prometheus text exposition written per tick.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-schema
Dump the active schema as JSON Schema (metric catalogue, selected
backend, semantic model, and rejection reasons per candidate), then
exit.
.TP
.B \-\-serve\fR[=\fIURI\fR]
Long-running Prometheus exposition endpoint (default
\fBprometheus://:9100\fR). Requires a build with libmicrohttpd.
.SS Diagnostics
.TP
.B \-\-probe
Check whether the eBPF backends can work here; the exit code
distinguishes kernel-too-old (2), not-permitted (3), built-without-
eBPF (4).
.TP
.B \-\-list\-backends
Print detected hardware capabilities, the selected backend per
metric, and the full ordered candidate lists with each candidate's
lineage (\fBstable\fR, \fBebpf\fR or \fBboth\fR) and the reason any
candidate was rejected, then exit. With
\fB\-\-experimental \-\-portable\-metrics\fR it lists the
\fBsystem.portable.*\fR chains too.
.SS Backend control
.TP
.B \-\-engine \fIENGINE\fR
Which backend lineage may run.
.B auto
(the default) picks, per metric, the highest-priority backend whose
probe passes: the eBPF backend where it works, the stable-ABI one
otherwise. In this mode an eBPF backend also steps aside when it
cannot keep the target's scope (a \fB\-p\fR/\fB\-\-pids\fR/\fB\-\-comm\fR
target, which eBPF attributes only per cgroup or system-wide) and, inside
a KVM guest, for \fBcpu\fR (its in-guest reading depends on the
sampling interval).
.B stable
(alias \fBc\fR, \fBv2.1\fR) runs only the stable-ABI C backends,
reproducing across-intp v2.1.
.B ebpf
(alias \fBv3.3\fR) runs only the eBPF lineage plus the resctrl
backends v3.3 shares, reproducing across-intp v3.3; it exits with status
2 when eBPF cannot load. Under a single lineage a metric that lineage
cannot serve reads unavailable instead of borrowing from the other.
.TP
.B \-\-disable\-backend \fINAME\fR
Skip a backend during selection. \fINAME\fR is a backend id
(\fBresctrl\fR) or \fImetric\fR\fB:\fR\fIid\fR (\fBmbw:resctrl_mbm\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-force\-backend \fIM\fR\fB:\fR\fIID\fR
Force a specific backend for metric \fIM\fR, regardless of
\fB\-\-engine\fR. \fIM\fR may be a core metric (\fBmbw:resctrl_mbm\fR) or,
with \fB\-\-portable\-metrics\fR, a portable one
(\fBschedlat:schedstat_pid\fR). Repeatable. Falls back to normal
selection if the forced backend's probe or init rejects. Backend ids
are listed by \fB\-\-list\-backends\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-disable\-metric \fIM\fR
Do not collect metric \fIM\fR at all.
.SS Hardware overrides
Normalization constants are autodetected; override when detection is
wrong or for cross-host comparability:
.B \-\-nic\-speed\-bps\fR,
.B \-\-mem\-bw\-max\-bps\fR,
.B \-\-llc\-size\-bytes\fR,
.B \-\-disk\-bw\-max\-bps\fR,
.B \-\-iface\fR,
.B \-\-disk\fR.
.SS Experimental
.TP
.B \-\-experimental
Opt in to experimental features (see
.BR "EXPERIMENTAL FEATURES" ).
.TP
.B \-\-portable\-metrics
(Experimental; requires \fB\-\-experimental\fR.) Additionally collect
the eight VM-portable metrics in the
.B system.portable.*
namespace:
.B schedlat
(run-queue/scheduling latency),
.B psi_mem
(PSI memory-capacity pressure; it does not register memory-bandwidth
saturation, so never read it as a bandwidth signal),
.B membw_est
(DRAM-bandwidth estimate, bytes/s; on network-heavy targets it also
counts the cache misses of intp's own eBPF network hooks, so treat it as
uncorroborated when
.B mbw
and
.B llcmr
read near zero),
.B psi_io
(PSI I/O pressure),
.B schedthr
(CFS throttling, cgroup-only),
.B steal
(hypervisor-stolen vCPU time),
.B psp
(involuntary preemption rate, events/s), and
.B idle_preempt
(idle-CPU takeover rate; eBPF-only, unavailable without eBPF or under
\fB\-\-engine=stable\fR). They appear in
.B jsonl
and
.B prometheus
output;
.B tsv\-legacy\-v1
keeps emitting exactly the seven frozen columns.
.SH EXPERIMENTAL FEATURES
Capabilities whose validation verdicts are not yet final ship behind a
deliberate fence (ADR-0008): they refuse to start without
.B \-\-experimental
and exit with status 2, naming what is unvalidated. Currently fenced:
.B \-\-target\-vm
(VM targeting) and
.B \-\-portable\-metrics
(the
.B system.portable.*
metric set), both pending the final verdicts of the portable-metrics
campaign (across-intp C26, C27; ADR-0012).
.PP
Every metric in
.B \-\-print\-schema
carries a
.B stability
field \(em
.B stable
for the seven core metrics,
.B experimental
for the portable set \(em so consumers can tell the trusted surface
from the opt-in one. Experimental output never enters
.B tsv\-legacy\-v1
or the frozen
.B system.interference.*
namespace, and may change or disappear in a minor release.
.SH SEMANTIC MODELS
Two backends of the same metric may measure different latent
quantities. The selected backend's model is declared in
.B \-\-print\-schema\fR.
Notably for
.BR blk :
the eBPF backend integrates per-request service time
(\fBsvctm_busy_fraction\fR, queue-depth aware); the procfs fallback
reads io_ticks (\fBio_ticks_util\fR, saturates on parallel NVMe);
the cgroup backend normalizes io.stat throughput
(\fBcgroup_io_throughput\fR). Compare values across hosts only within
the same model.
.SH PRIVILEGES
netp, nets, blk and cpu collect unprivileged. llcmr needs
perf_event access (root, CAP_PERFMON, or perf_event_paranoid \(<= 2
depending on scope). mbw and llcocc need resctrl (root) or uncore PMU
access. Missing privilege degrades the affected metric to
.I unavailable
with the reason recorded in the schema.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on runtime failure; 2 on usage errors. \fB\-\-probe\fR
uses 0/2/3/4/5 as described above.
.SH EXAMPLES
Profile the whole host once a second, JSONL on stdout:
.PP
.nf
    intp
.fi
.PP
Ten seconds of legacy TSV for an IADA-style consumer:
.PP
.nf
    intp \-f tsv\-legacy\-v1 \-i 1s \-d 10s
.fi
.PP
Profile one process, forcing the resctrl memory-bandwidth backend:
.PP
.nf
    sudo intp \-p 4242 \-\-force\-backend mbw:resctrl_mbm
.fi
.PP
Reproduce the two across-intp variants on one container, with all
fifteen metrics:
.PP
.nf
    sudo intp \-\-engine=stable \-\-experimental \-\-portable\-metrics \\
        \-c /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/docker\-ID.scope
    sudo intp \-\-engine=ebpf \-\-experimental \-\-portable\-metrics \\
        \-c /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/docker\-ID.scope
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR perf (1),
.BR pidstat (1),
.BR iostat (1)
